---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
---
<ProjectLayout
  slug="linkedin-reactions"
  title="Reactions"
  subtitle="A two-day craft intervention on LinkedIn's highest-frequency interaction"
  description="LinkedIn Reactions case study by Kenny Raymond — a two-day craft intervention on one of LinkedIn's highest-frequency interactions."
  ogImage="/Reactionshero.png"
>

  <!-- Hero image -->
  <section data-no-reveal class="project-hero-section px-3 md:px-36 lg:px-64">
    <img
      src="/Reactionshero.png"
      alt="LinkedIn Reactions hero"
      class="w-full h-auto rounded-[48px]"
    />
  </section>

  <!-- Overview -->
  <section class="px-3 md:px-36 lg:px-64 py-14 md:py-20">
    <h2 class="text-[2.8rem] md:text-[3.4rem] leading-[1] md:leading-[1.1] font-semibold text-white mt-4 mb-3 max-w-3xl mx-auto">Overview</h2>
    <p class="text-white text-2xl md:text-[1.69rem] leading-snug mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      Reactions are one of LinkedIn's highest-frequency interactions — millions of times a day, across every post in the feed. When the menu was being migrated to a new tech stack, I saw a low-effort, high-impact opportunity: tighten the craft before it shipped. This was a two-day side effort, not a dedicated workstream.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Approach -->
  <section class="px-3 md:px-36 lg:px-64 py-14 md:py-20">
    <h2 class="text-[2.8rem] md:text-[3.4rem] leading-[1] md:leading-[1.1] font-semibold text-white mt-4 mb-3 max-w-3xl mx-auto">Approach</h2>
    <p class="text-white text-2xl md:text-[1.69rem] leading-snug mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      Working within the existing asset library, I refined the icon shapes for better visual balance and consistency, and improved the selection motion to feel more tactile and intentional.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Before / After image -->
  <section class="px-0 md:px-0 py-10 md:py-14">
    <img
      src="/reactionsbeforeafter.png"
      alt="LinkedIn Reactions before and after comparison"
      class="w-full h-[45vh] md:h-auto object-cover object-center rounded-none md:rounded-[40px]"
    />
  </section>

  <!-- Impact -->
  <section class="px-3 md:px-36 lg:px-64 py-14 md:py-20">
    <h2 class="text-[2.8rem] md:text-[3.4rem] leading-[1] md:leading-[1.1] font-semibold text-white mt-4 mb-3 max-w-3xl mx-auto">Impact</h2>
    <p class="text-white text-2xl md:text-[1.69rem] leading-snug mx-auto mb-6 max-w-3xl">
      The improvements shipped with the migration — a quiet craft win on one of LinkedIn’s most-used surfaces.
    </p>
    <p class="text-white text-2xl md:text-[1.69rem] leading-snug mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      It also raised the team’s bar for craft. Motion polish at this level wasn’t routine at LinkedIn, and the project became a reference point in design all-hands — held up as an example of what including motion polish in your work can unlock. After it shipped, more designers started weaving motion thinking into their own projects. Craft compounds.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Reaction motion GIF -->
  <section class="px-0 md:px-0 py-10 md:py-14">
    <img
      src="/reactionMotion.gif"
      alt="LinkedIn Reactions interaction animation"
      class="w-full h-[70vh] md:h-auto object-cover object-center rounded-none md:rounded-[40px]"
    />
  </section>

</ProjectLayout>
